      <!-- The Pledge -->
      <trans-unit id="pledge.title" resname="pledge.title">
        <source>The Amélie Pledge (printed on every tin)</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pledge.text" resname="pledge.text">
        <source>This idea belongs to no one. Take it, build it, sell it — you owe me nothing, not even a reply. If you ever have an idea you won't build, give it to someone who will.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pledge.copy" resname="pledge.copy">
        <source>Copy pledge</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pledge.copied" resname="pledge.copied">
        <source>Copied</source>
      </trans-unit>

      <!-- The 5 Rules -->
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule1.title" resname="manifest.rule1.title">
        <source>The delivery is the gift, not the find</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule1.desc" resname="manifest.rule1.desc">
        <source>Ideas are cheap. Everyone has thirty. The gift only starts when you research a specific team capable of building it, and deliver a turn-key package.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule1.thumb" resname="manifest.rule1.thumb">
        <source>1h discovery, 1h validation, 2h recipient research (the 1:2 budget ratio).</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule1.pill" resname="manifest.rule1.pill">
        <source>Delivery</source>
      </trans-unit>

      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule2.title" resname="manifest.rule2.title">
        <source>Sign your name, demand nothing</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule2.desc" resname="manifest.rule2.desc">
        <source>Your name underneath, CC0 above it. No equity demands, no mandatory attribution, zero expectation of a response. The Kula ring thrives because the gift travels onward, not backward.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule2.thumb" resname="manifest.rule2.thumb">
        <source>Explicitly grant permission not to reply.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule2.pill" resname="manifest.rule2.pill">
        <source>Sign &amp; CC0</source>
      </trans-unit>

      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule3.title" resname="manifest.rule3.title">
        <source>The Phone Booth Rule (Strictly no follow-up)</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule3.desc" resname="manifest.rule3.desc">
        <source>Place the tin box in the phone booth and disappear. No follow-up emails, no "checking in", no LinkedIn requests. If you ask for feedback, it wasn't a gift — it was an unpaid pitch.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule3.thumb" resname="manifest.rule3.thumb">
        <source>Send once. Then erase from your mental to-do list.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule3.pill" resname="manifest.rule3.pill">
        <source>No Follow-up</source>
      </trans-unit>

      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule4.title" resname="manifest.rule4.title">
        <source>If they didn't ask, deliver tools, not homework</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule4.desc" resname="manifest.rule4.desc">
        <source>Unsolicited ideas without code belong only to entities with budgets and a mandate to build (companies, academic chairs, public funds). Never burden volunteer open source maintainers with feature requests; maintainers only receive ready-to-merge pull requests.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule4.thumb" resname="manifest.rule4.thumb">
        <source>Never give volunteer maintainers unpaid homework.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule4.pill" resname="manifest.rule4.pill">
        <source>Tools Only</source>
      </trans-unit>

      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule5.title" resname="manifest.rule5.title">
        <source>Don't make gifting an excuse not to build</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule5.desc" resname="manifest.rule5.desc">
        <source>Gifting ideas feels like building, but it isn't. Keep a maximum of two personal projects a year and finish them thoroughly. Gift everything else away so it doesn't rot.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule5.thumb" resname="manifest.rule5.thumb">
        <source>Build max 2 personal projects. Gift the remaining 19.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="manifest.rule5.pill" resname="manifest.rule5.pill">
        <source>Build Max 2</source>
      </trans-unit>

      <!-- 6 Pillars of Amélie Emails -->
      <trans-unit id="pillar.title" resname="pillar.title">
        <source>The 6 Pillars of an Amélie Gift Email</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.1.title" resname="pillar.1.title">
        <source>1. Clear Gift Subject Line</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.1.desc" resname="pillar.1.desc">
        <source>Explicitly marked as a free gift; zero sales hype, zero clickbait.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.2.title" resname="pillar.2.title">
        <source>2. The 1:20 Ratio Relief</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.2.desc" resname="pillar.2.desc">
        <source>Immediately clarifies who writes and why 19 out of 20 ideas are given away.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.3.title" resname="pillar.3.title">
        <source>3. Proof of Sincere Research</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.3.desc" resname="pillar.3.desc">
        <source>Cites the recipient's recent paper or software tool (no blast spam).</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.4.title" resname="pillar.4.title">
        <source>4. The Tin Link &amp; One-Pager</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.4.desc" resname="pillar.4.desc">
        <source>1 page with architecture, first milestone (Ticket #1), and critical failure point.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.5.title" resname="pillar.5.title">
        <source>5. Unconditional CC0 Release</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.5.desc" resname="pillar.5.desc">
        <source>Unconditionally public domain: take it, build it, sell it, zero royalties.</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.6.title" resname="pillar.6.title">
        <source>6. Strict Phone Booth Rule</source>
      </trans-unit>
      <trans-unit id="pillar.6.desc" resname="pillar.6.desc">
        <source>Explicit permission not to reply; promise never to follow up or nag.</source>
      </trans-unit>

